{"id": "ft63_lock_escalation_deadlock_risk", "expected_regex": "(?i)(?=.*deadlock.*yes|yes.*deadlock)(?=.*(?:SKU001|SKU002).{0,100}(?:SKU001|SKU002))(?=.*(?:lock.*order|order.*lock|circular)).*", "exemplar": "Deadlock possible: Yes
Lock escalation scenario: Transaction A locks SKU001, then SKU002. Transaction B locks SKU002 (with UPDATE SET), then tries to lock SKU001. Both transactions hold row locks on different rows and wait for each other's locks, creating a circular wait. Lock escalation may promote row locks to page or table locks, exacerbating the contention.
Prevention strategy: Enforce consistent lock acquisition order across all transactions?always lock SKU001 before SKU002. Use stored procedures or ORM constructs to enforce this globally. Alternatively, use pessimistic locking with ordered sequences.", "counter_example": "Deadlock possible: No, because the transactions update different rows. Prevention strategy: Add more rows to the inventory table to reduce lock contention."}
{"id": "ft64_connection_pool_saturation_symptom", "expected_regex": "(?i)(?=.*queue.*timeout|timeout.*queue)(?=.*30|waiting.*30)(?=.*increase.*pool|pool.*wait|wait.*queue).*", "exemplar": "Root cause: The queue_timeout (30s) is configured but requests in the queue may be waiting too long. With 15 concurrent requests and pool_size=10, 5 requests enter the queue. If connection handling is slow, the queued requests time out after queue_timeout, but the symptom shows one timing out at 25s?likely because the connection_timeout (5s) occurs before the request even gets a connection.
