{"id": "ft106_x86_tso_store_buffering", "expected_regex": "(?im)^\s*(?:answe<path>\s*)?\*{0,2}OUTCOME_POSSIBLE\b", "exemplar": "OUTCOME_POSSIBLE

The x86-TSO (Total Store Order) memory model permits store-buffering behavior. In the store-buffering (SB) litmus test, Core 0's write to x can be buffered while it reads y (before y is written, so r1=0). Simultaneously, Core 1's write to y is buffered while it reads x (before x is written, so r2=0). This is a documented allowed behavior in x86-TSO: each core has a write buffer, so the outcome {r1=0, r2=0} is possible.", "counter_example": "OUTCOME_IMPOSSIBLE. x86 processors execute instructions in strict sequential consistency order, so if Core 0 writes to x, that write must complete before Core 1 can read it. The memory barrier ensures ordering, making both r1 and r2 non-zero."}
