{"id": "ft50_state_machine_partial_recovery", "expected_regex": "(?i)(?=.*STEP.*3|step.*3|resume)(?=.*(?:idempotent|partial|retain|recover))(?=.*trade.*off).*", "exemplar": "Resume from STEP3. Tradeoff: Resuming from STEP1 is safer but wasteful (re-does work). STEP3 is riskier but faster if the operation is idempotent (e.g., re-releasing resources is safe if well-designed). Best: make STEP3 idempotent via checkpointing.", "counter_example": "Always resume from STEP1 to avoid any inconsistency."}
{"id": "ft51_unicode_emoji_byte_length", "expected_regex": "(?i)(?=.*7|8|9|10|11)(?=.*(?:emoji|\ud83d\udc4b|multi.*byte|UTF.*8)).*", "exemplar": "len(text) = 7 (6 ASCII chars + 1 emoji = 7 characters). len(text.encode('utf-8')) = 11 bytes (5 ASCII = 5 bytes + emoji \\xF0\\x9F\\x91\\x8B = 4 bytes). They differ because Unicode characters can take multiple bytes in UTF-8.", "counter_example": "Both len() and len(bytes) should return the same value."}
{"id": "ft52_unicode_combining_character", "expected_regex": "(?i)(?=.*decomposed?|composed?|NFC|NFD|combining|normalize)(?=.*(?:Fix|casefold|normalize)).*", "exemplar": "The strings are different: composed \u00e9 is U+00E9 (1 char) vs. decomposed e + acute (2 chars). They hash differently and == returns False. Fix: Use unicodedata.normalize('NFC', text) before comparison to normalize both to the same form.", "counter_example": "The strings should be equal because they look identical when displayed."}
{"id": "ft53_cache_stampede_concurrent", "expected_regex": "(?i)(?=.*stampede|thundering|herd)(?=.*(?:multiple|simultaneous|all.*hit))(?=.*(?:lock|shedule|drip|single.*update)).*", "exemplar": "Problem: Cache stampede (thundering herd)\u2014all concurrent requests miss simultaneously and all query the database, creating a spike. Solution: Use a lock or 'shedding' pattern\u2014first request gets the lock and refreshes the cache, others wait and then use the refreshed value.", "counter_example": "The problem is that the database is too slow to handle multiple queries."}
{"id": "ft54_cache_double_invalidation", "expected_regex": "(?i)(?=.*Scenario.*1|scenario.*1)(?=.*(?:L1.*L2|L2.*stale|fail.*L2)).*", "exemplar": "Scenario 1 (invalidate L1 then L2) is safer. If failure occurs after L1 invalidation, L2 still has the stale value. New requests go to L1 (miss), hit L2 (stale), and see old data. Scenario 2 (invalidate L2 then L1) risks invalidating L2 then failing before L1 invalidation, leaving L1 with stale data that bypasses L2 check.", "counter_example": "Scenario 2 is safer because the in-memory cache is checked first."}
